  "query_preview": "..Conditions are trending drier and hotter as a ridge\ncurrently centered over New Mexico continues to march toward the\nwest. It will also strengthen and elongate as it treks to the\nwest. This will keep the drying trend going as it cuts off the\nmonsoon moisture tap with a weak westerly flow developing.\nAdditionally, temperatures will approach near record territory for\nportions of the CWA. 700mb temperatures peak on Saturday with the\n50th percentile ~20C and the 75th percentile ~21C. When we\nrecorded our all-time record high of 109F a few weeks ago our\n700mb temp also broke the all-time high of 21C. Our upper level \ntemperatures will be close to that, but given weaker southerly \nflow we should go to a northerly lake-breeze sooner than the day \nwe hit 109F. \n\nOur forecast high on Saturday will likely be in the 103-106 \nrange. 110F is the current forecast for St. George. Overnight \nrecoveries will also be warm, however the drier airmass should at \nleast allow temperatures to drop into the 70s, rather than the 80s\nwe saw this past week when cloud cover and moisture kept \novernight lows well into the 80s. Regardless, the heat risk will \nbe elevated this week. Smoke from nearby wildfires will need to be\nmonitored as the weekend progresses to see if it influences the \ntemperatures, but winds favor less smoke given the \nsoutherly/westerly orientation and most fire are to our northwest.\n\nA trough passing by to our north late this weekend will bring a\nweak cold front through the area with a dip in temperatures closer\nto normal with the most noticeable drop across northern Utah. As\nthis trough approaches, the pressure gradient will tighten with \nsouthwesterly flow increasing. This combined with low RH will\nelevate the critical fire weather conditions, particularly across\ncentral and eastern Utah where winds are expected to be strongest\n(25-35 mph gusts).\n\nMoisture will slowly start to creep back into southern Utah\ntowards the end of next week as the ridge gradually tracks back to\nthe east, but guidance is showing a variance of several days so\nexact timing remains uncertain.",
